sth:connection_check sth:broker_port_description "The default MQTT communication port is 1883."@en ;
    sth:mqtt_default_communication_port "1883" ;
    sth:question "Under what circumstances does the Smart Tool Holder need to be rescanned?"@en,
        "When does the Smart Tool Holder need to be rescanned?"@en ;
    sth:rescan_condition "When RSSI ≤ -50 dB, signal strength is insufficient and may cause connection interruption or incomplete data, so the Smart Tool Holder must be rescanned."@en ;
    sth:rescan_condition_query sth:rssi_insufficient_rescan_condition_direct_qa ;
    sth:rssi_insufficient_rescan_condition_direct_qa "When the signal strength between the Smart Tool Holder and Router is insufficient (RSSI ≤ -50 dB), the Smart Tool Holder must be rescanned."@en .

sth:connection_check_detailed a sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "Detailed Operation Instructions - Connection Check"@en ;
    sth:description "Connection Check. Figure 32, Connection Check page. Operation procedure: 1. Settings and Scan Operations (Figure 33). RESET DEFAULT resets parameters to system defaults by reading initial settings from InitSTH.py. APPLY UPDATES saves changed connection parameters to InitSTH_u.json and displays all changes in Parameter Updates. LOAD STHSCAN loads the Smart Tool Holder scanning program (STHScan.exe). RE-SCAN starts the STH scan to identify currently available Smart Tool Holders. UNLOAD SCAN terminates and exits the scanning program. 2. Available Smart Tool Holders and Scan Settings (Figure 34). Available STHs displays scanned Smart Tool Holder MAC addresses and corresponding Port numbers, up to five sets. STH Scan: WIFI Router Used? selects whether scanning is performed through a Wi-Fi Router. First Port for Scanning sets the starting connection port. Total STHs sets the maximum number of Smart Tool Holders to scan. Max Scan Time sets the maximum duration in seconds for each scan; these settings are saved in InitSTH_u.json. 3. Connection Port Settings (Figure 35). Assign/Reset Port Setting: Reset All Ports of STHs selects whether to reset all scanned STH connection ports. Auto-Assign New Port selects whether to automatically assign a new connection port. Manual Assign selects manual assignment of different connection ports. 4. Networking and MQTT Settings (Figure 36). Networking (TCP/IP): STH Router Name sets the Router name used to connect the Smart Tool Holder. Edge IP Address sets the IP address of the Edge computer. Web Address and Web Port configure web communication between the UI and system. Message Broking (MQTT): Remote Control? sets whether remote control of the Smart Tool Holder is allowed. Is a Local Broker? selects whether a local MQTT Broker is used. Message Broker Address and Broker Port set the MQTT Broker address and communication port. Topic of Syn/Scan/pdata/log sets the MQTT topic names for Syn, Scan, pdata, and Log to ensure proper message transmission for each function module. 5. Updates and Scan Records (Figure 37). Parameter Updates displays all Connection Check and Save Settings content in JSON format after APPLY UPDATES to verify that changes were applied correctly. Scan Logs displays the STHScan.exe runtime log after LOAD STHSCAN."@en ;
    sth:has_check_rule sth:sufficient_signal_strength_judgment ;
    sth:rescan_operation "Press RE-SCAN to rescan Smart Tool Holders and check which Smart Tool Holders are currently available."@en .

sth:connection_check_quick a sth:process_step ;
    rdfs:label "Quick operation procedure - Connection Check"@en ;
    sth:description "Connection Check. On the Connection Check page (Figure 8), place ST_route near the CNC machine in the Networking (TCP/IP settings) area to ensure a stable Wi-Fi connection and configure the correct IP address. Press LOAD STHSCAN to load the STH scanning program and use RE-SCAN to ensure that all Smart Tool Holders are successfully scanned and connected. Signal strength can be viewed on the Real-Time Monitoring page. RSSI > -50 dB indicates sufficient signal strength between the Smart Tool Holder and Router. RSSI ≤ -50 dB indicates insufficient signal strength, which may cause connection interruption or incomplete data and requires rescanning the Smart Tool Holder. Figure 8, Connection Check page."@en ;
    sth:has_check_rule sth:sufficient_signal_strength_judgment ;
    sth:rescan_condition "When RSSI ≤ -50 dB, signal strength is insufficient and may cause connection interruption or incomplete data, so the Smart Tool Holder must be rescanned."@en ;
    sth:rssi_insufficient_rescan_condition_direct_qa "When the signal strength between the Smart Tool Holder and Router is insufficient (RSSI ≤ -50 dB), the Smart Tool Holder must be rescanned."@en .

sth:connection_latency_analysis a sth:analysis ;
    rdfs:label "Connection Latency Analysis"@en ;
    sth:conclusion "The Smart Tool Holder can operate stably for about 7 hours; communication remains stable at 10000 rpm and does not constitute communication latency."@en ;
    sth:description "Connection Latency Analysis. Execution time: 04/22 14:37–04/22 17:45, 2025. Conditions: spindle speeds 9000 and 10000 rpm; feed 0 mm/min; duration 10 min/cycle. Description: every 0.4 seconds the Smart Tool Holder sends accumulated sensor measurements and the previous packet transmission time (TTOLP). The time required to transmit a packet during the previous connection is sent with the next packet, so TTOLP varies significantly when the connection is unstable. Results: with different Smart Tool Holders at spindle speeds of 9000 and 10000 RPM over 10-minute communication connections, average TTOLP is 30–50 ms and the maximum is less than 90 ms. At 10000 rpm, communication time remains stable and does not constitute communication latency. Execution time: 5/11 21:27–5/12 06:45, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 0 rpm; maximum voltage 4.11 V; minimum voltage 2.67 V; duration >7 hr. Description: the Smart Tool Holder operates on a lithium battery. During continuous communication, remaining battery power gradually decreases. This analysis examines the dependence between communication latency and remaining battery level. Results: during the first 7 hours of continuous communication, TTOLP remains below 100 ms. At 8–9 hours, as remaining battery power rapidly declines, TTOLP rises from 100 ms to 2000 ms and can reach 4.8 s. The results show that the Smart Tool Holder can operate stably for 7 hours."@en ;
    sth:power_test_result "During the first 7 hours of continuous communication, TTOLP remains below 100 ms. At 8–9 hours, as remaining battery power rapidly decreases, TTOLP rises from 100 ms to 2000 ms and can reach 4.8 s. Stable Smart Tool Holder operating time is 7 hours."@en ;
    sth:result "In tests at 9000 and 10000 RPM with a 10-minute communication connection, average TTOLP is 30–50 ms and the maximum is less than 90 ms. During the first 7 hours of continuous communication, TTOLP remains below 100 ms; at 8–9 hours, as remaining battery power rapidly decreases, TTOLP rises from 100 ms to 2000 ms and can reach a maximum of 4.8 s."@en ;
    sth:spindle_speed_test_result "With different Smart Tool Holders at spindle speeds of 9000 and 10000 RPM and a 10-minute communication connection, the average TTOLP is 30–50 ms and the maximum is less than 90 ms. The results show that communication time remains stable at 10000 rpm and does not constitute communication latency."@en ;
    sth:ttolp_description "The Smart Tool Holder sends accumulated sensor measurements and the previous packet transmission time (TTOLP) every 0.4 seconds. The time required to transmit a packet during the previous connection is reported with the next packet, so TTOLP varies significantly when the connection is unstable."@en .

sth:connection_settings a sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "Connection Settings"@en ;
    sth:description "Connection Settings. Tool holder network settings include S0 and S1. S0 is the shipping default with SSID ST_route and Port 6500. S1 is user-defined and can be configured in Configuration Mode. After entering Transmission Mode, the STH uses S1 for the first default 30 seconds. If it cannot connect using S1 within the default 30-second wait, it automatically switches to S0; the maximum default wait is 30 seconds. If no connection is established for more than 60 seconds in Transmission Mode, the STH enters Sleep Mode. Table 5, Smart Tool Holder Connection Settings: S0 / S1. Router SSID: ST_route / ST_route[1, 2.., n]. Router Password: 123456abc / User defined. Connection Port: 6500 / [6501, 6502, …, 6510]. Edge IP: 10.0.0.103 / [10.0.0.104, …, 10.0.0.123]."@en ;
    sth:has_setting sth:s0_setting,
        sth:s1_setting .

sth:continue_synchronization_command a sth:command ;
    rdfs:label "Continue synchronization"@en ;
    sth:EdgeCommand """syn
""" ;
    sth:WSBResponse """syn:OK
""" .

