sth:save_settings a sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "Save Settings"@en ;
    sth:convert_force_only_checkbox_item "Transfer Force Only?" ;
    sth:data_parsing_settings_description "Record the Raw Data? selects whether to record the Smart Tool Holder raw data; Save a Parsed File? selects whether to convert the force information detected by the Smart Tool Holder from TXT format to CSV format; Write Mode of CSV specifies whether new force information is appended to an existing CSV file; Transfer Force Only? selects whether only the force information is translated from the raw data."@en ;
    sth:description "Save Settings. Figure 38, Save Settings page. Save Settings page procedure. 1. Plan management and reset (Figure 39). RESET DEFAULT: resets parameters to default values by loading initial settings from InitSTH.py, allowing quick restoration of system defaults. APPLY UPDATES: after confirming the enabled STH status, saves these updates to JSON for later use. 2. Active Smart Tool Holder (Active STH) (Figure 40). STH_version displays the version of the currently enabled Smart Tool Holder. Active STH-MAC displays its MAC address. Tool ID displays the tool ID currently bound to the enabled STH. Total channels displays the total number of output channels. Port displays the Smart Tool Holder connection port. 3. File Location settings (Figure 41). Root Folder Location sets the Virtual Tool Room data root directory. Raw Data Folder Location sets the directory for Smart Tool Holder raw data. Log File Location sets the directory for Virtual Tool Room abnormal-event log files; generated logs record STH and Node-Red system abnormalities and errors in CSV and TXT format for troubleshooting and diagnosis. Output Path sets the default STH output path for parsed data. 4. Data Parsing (TXT -> CSV) settings (Figure 42). Record the Raw Data? selects whether to record Smart Tool Holder raw data. Save a Parsed File? selects whether detected force information is converted from TXT to CSV. Write Mode of CSV specifies whether new force information is appended to an existing CSV file. Transfer Force Only? selects whether only force information is converted from Smart Tool Holder raw data. Waiting Time for Parsing (Sec) sets the wait time in seconds for each batch of raw-data parsing. Backup Raw Files to Another Folder? selects whether raw data is backed up to another folder. Threshold for Keep Data (Nm) sets the minimum force-recording threshold; only values above it are recorded. No. of TXT Files in a CSV File sets the number of TXT files included in each CSV file and can be adjusted with a slider. 5. Coefficient settings (Figure 43). Time Interval of Forces (sec) sets the time interval of force data. Ratios for Force sets calibration ratios for different force channels. Calibration of Channels sets each channel's calibration value. AVAILABLESTH writes the force-calibration and signal-calibration multipliers for each channel of the currently enabled STH-MAC into the settings; if the corresponding MAC is not found, default values are used. 6. Parameter Updates and Logs (Figure 44). After APPLY UPDATES is pressed, Parameter Updates displays all Connection Check and Save Settings content in JSON format to help confirm that changes were applied correctly."@en ;
    sth:has_setting sth:available_sth_coefficient_file,
        sth:channel_calibration,
        sth:csv_write_mode,
        sth:force_ratio,
        sth:force_time_interval,
        sth:log_file_location,
        sth:minimum_recording_threshold,
        sth:output_path,
        sth:parsing_wait_time,
        sth:raw_data_directory_location,
        sth:root_directory_location,
        sth:txt_count_per_csv,
        sth:whether_to_back_up_raw_data,
        sth:whether_to_convert_force_only,
        sth:whether_to_record_raw_data,
        sth:whether_to_save_parsed_files ;
    sth:record_raw_data_checkbox_item "Record the Raw Data?" ;
    sth:txt_to_csv_checkbox_item "Save a Parsed File?" .

sth:sensitivity_analysis a sth:analysis,
        sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    sth:conclusion "The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect wear changes in general, small-diameter, and ultra-small-diameter machining."@en ;
    sth:description "Sensitivity Analysis. General finishing test: machining/execution time 4/22 18:37–4/22 18:38, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 800 mm/min; depth of cut 1 mm; width of cut 1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 10 mm; length 8 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder can be used for high-speed finishing. This analysis evaluates general finishing by side milling with a typical end mill. Results: comparing actual torque from the Smart Tool Holder with simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remained in good condition and torque remained about 0.09±0.005 Nm. For general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection. Small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 5/5 17:11–5/5 17:12, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 180 mm/min; depth of cut 0.5 mm; width of cut 0.1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 2 mm; length 15 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-material-removal operations where torque-detection precision is critical as the tool wears. Results: in a single pass, the actual torque gradually increased within 30 sec as the tool wore, by 0.001 Nm relative to the simulated value. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect short-term wear changes of a 2 mm tool. Ultra-small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 4/28 16:20–4/28 16:42, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 100, 400, 1600 mm/min; depth of cut 0.015 mm; width of cut 0.5 mm; duration 22 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 0.5 mm; length 8 mm; 2 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-removal operations with progressive tool wear, where torque-detection precision is critical. This analysis evaluates ultra-small-diameter finishing at different feed rates. Results: with a 0.5 mm tool diameter and increasing feed rate, the actual torque relative to simulation increased by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second, and 0.008 Nm in the third as the tool wore. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect ultra-small-diameter tool wear changes."@en ;
    sth:general_finishing_result "Comparing the actual torque obtained from the Smart Tool Holder with the simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remains in good condition and torque stays at 0.09±0.005 Nm. For general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection."@en ;
    sth:has_subsection sth:sensitivity_analysis_general_finishing,
        sth:sensitivity_analysis_small_diameter,
        sth:sensitivity_analysis_ultra_small_diameter ;
    sth:result "For general finishing (tool diameter 10 mm), good torque detection is provided; for a small-diameter tool (2 mm), a 0.001 Nm increase can be observed within 30 seconds; for an ultra-small-diameter tool (0.5 mm), relative to the simulated value, increases of 0.001, 0.002, and 0.008 Nm can be observed at different feed rates."@en ;
    sth:small_diameter_finishing_result "For single-pass machining with a 2 mm tool diameter, as the tool gradually wears, the actual value can be observed to increase within 30 sec, with a relative increase of 0.001 Nm. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect short-term wear changes of small-diameter tools."@en ;
    sth:ultra_small_diameter_finishing_result "With a 0.5 mm tool diameter and gradually increasing feed rate, as tool wear increases, the actual value relative to the simulated value increases by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second segment, and 0.008 Nm in the third segment. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect wear changes of ultra-small-diameter tools."@en .

sth:sensitivity_analysis_general_finishing a sth:analysis ;
    rdfs:label "Sensitivity Analysis - General Finishing"@en ;
    sth:description "Sensitivity Analysis. General finishing test: machining/execution time 4/22 18:37–4/22 18:38, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 800 mm/min; depth of cut 1 mm; width of cut 1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 10 mm; length 8 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder can be used for high-speed finishing. This analysis evaluates general finishing by side milling with a typical end mill. Results: comparing actual torque from the Smart Tool Holder with simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remained in good condition and torque remained about 0.09±0.005 Nm. For general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection. Small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 5/5 17:11–5/5 17:12, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 180 mm/min; depth of cut 0.5 mm; width of cut 0.1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 2 mm; length 15 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-material-removal operations where torque-detection precision is critical as the tool wears. Results: in a single pass, the actual torque gradually increased within 30 sec as the tool wore, by 0.001 Nm relative to the simulated value. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect short-term wear changes of a 2 mm tool. Ultra-small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 4/28 16:20–4/28 16:42, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 100, 400, 1600 mm/min; depth of cut 0.015 mm; width of cut 0.5 mm; duration 22 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 0.5 mm; length 8 mm; 2 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-removal operations with progressive tool wear, where torque-detection precision is critical. This analysis evaluates ultra-small-diameter finishing at different feed rates. Results: with a 0.5 mm tool diameter and increasing feed rate, the actual torque relative to simulation increased by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second, and 0.008 Nm in the third as the tool wore. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect ultra-small-diameter tool wear changes."@en ;
    sth:result "Comparing the actual torque obtained from the Smart Tool Holder with the simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remains in good condition and torque stays at 0.09±0.005 Nm. The results show that, for general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection."@en .

sth:sensitivity_analysis_small_diameter a sth:analysis ;
    rdfs:label "Sensitivity Analysis - Small Diameter"@en ;
    sth:description "Sensitivity Analysis. General finishing test: machining/execution time 4/22 18:37–4/22 18:38, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 800 mm/min; depth of cut 1 mm; width of cut 1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 10 mm; length 8 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder can be used for high-speed finishing. This analysis evaluates general finishing by side milling with a typical end mill. Results: comparing actual torque from the Smart Tool Holder with simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remained in good condition and torque remained about 0.09±0.005 Nm. For general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection. Small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 5/5 17:11–5/5 17:12, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 180 mm/min; depth of cut 0.5 mm; width of cut 0.1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 2 mm; length 15 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-material-removal operations where torque-detection precision is critical as the tool wears. Results: in a single pass, the actual torque gradually increased within 30 sec as the tool wore, by 0.001 Nm relative to the simulated value. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect short-term wear changes of a 2 mm tool. Ultra-small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 4/28 16:20–4/28 16:42, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 100, 400, 1600 mm/min; depth of cut 0.015 mm; width of cut 0.5 mm; duration 22 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 0.5 mm; length 8 mm; 2 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-removal operations with progressive tool wear, where torque-detection precision is critical. This analysis evaluates ultra-small-diameter finishing at different feed rates. Results: with a 0.5 mm tool diameter and increasing feed rate, the actual torque relative to simulation increased by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second, and 0.008 Nm in the third as the tool wore. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect ultra-small-diameter tool wear changes."@en ;
    sth:result "Comparing the actual torque obtained from the Smart Tool Holder with the simulated torque, during a single machining pass the actual value gradually rises within 30 sec as the tool wears, with a relative increase of 0.001 Nm. The results show that the Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect wear changes of a small-diameter tool (2 mm) within a short period (30 sec)."@en .

sth:sensitivity_analysis_ultra_small_diameter a sth:analysis ;
    rdfs:label "Sensitivity Analysis - Ultra-Small Diameter"@en ;
    sth:description "Sensitivity Analysis. General finishing test: machining/execution time 4/22 18:37–4/22 18:38, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 800 mm/min; depth of cut 1 mm; width of cut 1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 10 mm; length 8 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder can be used for high-speed finishing. This analysis evaluates general finishing by side milling with a typical end mill. Results: comparing actual torque from the Smart Tool Holder with simulated torque, after three machining passes the tool remained in good condition and torque remained about 0.09±0.005 Nm. For general finishing with a 10 mm tool diameter, the Smart Tool Holder provides good torque detection. Small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 5/5 17:11–5/5 17:12, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 180 mm/min; depth of cut 0.5 mm; width of cut 0.1 mm; duration 1 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 2 mm; length 15 mm; 4 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-material-removal operations where torque-detection precision is critical as the tool wears. Results: in a single pass, the actual torque gradually increased within 30 sec as the tool wore, by 0.001 Nm relative to the simulated value. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect short-term wear changes of a 2 mm tool. Ultra-small-diameter finishing test: machining/execution time 4/28 16:20–4/28 16:42, 2025. Conditions: spindle speed 10000 rpm; feed 100, 400, 1600 mm/min; depth of cut 0.015 mm; width of cut 0.5 mm; duration 22 min; workpiece material FDAC. Tool conditions: diameter 0.5 mm; length 8 mm; 2 flutes. Description: the Smart Tool Holder is suitable for high-speed, high-precision machining, especially small-diameter, low-removal operations with progressive tool wear, where torque-detection precision is critical. This analysis evaluates ultra-small-diameter finishing at different feed rates. Results: with a 0.5 mm tool diameter and increasing feed rate, the actual torque relative to simulation increased by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second, and 0.008 Nm in the third as the tool wore. The Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect ultra-small-diameter tool wear changes."@en ;
    sth:result "With a 0.5 mm tool diameter and gradually increasing feed rate, as tool wear increases, the actual value relative to the simulated value increases by 0.001 Nm in the first segment, 0.002 Nm in the second segment, and 0.008 Nm in the third segment. The results show that the Smart Tool Holder can effectively detect wear changes of ultra-small-diameter tools."@en .

