sth:data_collection_quick a sth:process_step ;
    rdfs:label "Data Collection"@en ;
    sth:auto_stop_setting "Stop In Time(s)" ;
    sth:blue_led_status_query sth:smart_tool_holder_blue_led_status ;
    sth:description "Data Collection. Go to the Real-Time Monitoring page (Figure 10) and press LOAD ALL to start the STH connection and data-processing programs. The Smart Tool Holder LED turns blue, indicating normal connection. Confirm good connection status, then press START RECORD to record STH sensor data as TXT files. Stop In Time(s) can specify the automatic recording stop time. Recording Time displays the recorded duration; ensure that the recording covers the entire machining process to be observed. Figure 10, Data Collection page."@en ;
    sth:display_blue_led "Normally connected."@en ;
    sth:record_sensor_data_method "Go to the Real-Time Monitoring page and press LOAD ALL to start the STH connection and data-processing programs. After confirming a good connection, press START RECORD to record STH sensor data (TXT files). Stop In Time(s) can specify the automatic recording stop time. Recording Time displays the recorded duration."@en ;
    sth:start_recording_button "START RECORD" ;
    sth:startup_procedure "Press LOAD ALL to start the STH connection and data-processing programs."@en ;
    sth:what_does_blue_led_on_smart_tool_holder_mean "A blue LED on the Smart Tool Holder means the TCP packet channel is enabled and the Smart Tool Holder is in Transmission Mode or normally connected."@en .

sth:history_review a sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "History Review"@en ;
    sth:add_control_limits_method "After selecting Folder and File, click ADD UCL LCL to add upper and lower control limits (UCL/LCL) to the selected CSV file. The output filename is formed by appending _processed to the original filename."@en,
        "After selecting Folder and File, click ADD UCL LCL to add upper and lower control limits (UCL/LCL) to the selected CSV file. The output filename is the original filename + _processed."@en ;
    sth:add_ucl_lcl_function "Add upper and lower control limits to the selected file."@en ;
    sth:control_limit_output_filename_rule "original filename + _processed"@en ;
    sth:data_review_start_and_end_times "Start Time and End Time display the start and end times of the data review, allowing the exact test time range to be identified."@en ;
    sth:description "History Review. Figure 55, History Review page. Operation procedure: 1. Plan and Folder Settings (Figure 56). Current Plan displays the absolute path of the current plan file. Folder selects an available folder under C:/VTR/RawData/output and loads the corresponding test data. File selects an available data file in the current directory for review analysis and adding upper/lower control limits. ADD UCL LCL: after selecting Folder and File, click ADD UCL LCL to add UCL/LCL to the selected CSV file; the output filename is the original filename + _processed. Time Interval selects the available data file in the current directory for review analysis and adding control limits. 2. File Merge (Figure 57). LOAD FILEMERGE loads the file-merge function to combine multiple test results for further analysis. ENABLE MERGE starts merging selected file data. PAUSE MERGE pauses merging so users can perform other operations or inspect data. UNLOAD MERGE terminates all merge processes and returns the system to the non-merge state. 3. Summary (Figure 58). Start Time and End Time display the start and end times of data review for precise identification of the test range. Time Index (from) adjusts the time bar for displayed review points. Time Zoom adjusts the review time range for detailed analysis of a specific period. CH1 Max, CH2 Max, CH3 Max, and CH4 Max display the maximum voltage values (mV) of channels CH1, CH2, BendingX, and BendingY. Quality displays data-quality status: 1 means differences among all channels are within the acceptable range and quality passes; -1 means a difference exceeds tolerance, quality fails, and further inspection is required. 4. Add as Benchmark (Figure 59). SCAN PLAN rescans plan files under C:/VTR/Plans for selection as benchmarks. Select a Plan selects an available plan file as the benchmark for subsequent tests. AS BENCHMARK sets the selected plan as a benchmark for comparison with other test data. 5. Charts (Figure 60). Torque(Nm), Bending X(KNm), Bending Y(KNm), CH1(mV), and CH2(mV) display channel data in the current file as line charts to visualize torque and bending-force variation. UCL: Upper Control Limit. LCL: Lower Control Limit."@en ;
    sth:end_time "End Time displays the end time of the data review."@en ;
    sth:how_to_confirm_data_review_start_and_end_times "On the History Review page, view Start Time and End Time in the Summary area."@en ;
    sth:quality_judgment "Quality displays the data quality status. A value of 1 means the differences among all channels are within the acceptable range and quality passes; -1 means there are differences outside the tolerance range, quality fails, and further inspection is required."@en ;
    sth:start_time "Start Time displays the start time of the data review."@en .

sth:initial_operation a sth:section ;
    rdfs:label "Initial operation"@en ;
    sth:MQTTPort "1883" ;
    sth:communication_setting_steps "Open Control Panel, go to System and Security, open Windows Defender Firewall, select Advanced settings, add a new Inbound Rule, select Port, configure TCP and specific local port 1883, select Allow the connection, apply the rule to Domain, Private, and Public, and complete the rule setup."@en ;
    sth:description "Initial Operation. Install Python and confirm environment variables: after installing Python, check whether python.exe exists in the Python environment-variable path (Figures 12 and 13) to ensure normal system operation. Figure 12, Python environment variables. Figure 13, python.exe under the environment-variable path. MQTT prerequisite settings: install Mosquitto as the MQTT broker from mosquitto.org (Figure 14). Before using MQTT, add an inbound rule in Windows Defender Firewall to allow Port 1883 communication so the computer can enable the MQTT service (Figure 15). (1) Download the required version from the Mosquitto website. Figures 14 and 15, MQTT prerequisite settings. (2) Search for Control Panel, open it, and select System and Security (Figure 16). (3) Open Windows Defender Firewall (Figure 17). (4) Select Advanced settings (Figure 18). (5) Select Inbound Rules and click New Rule (Figure 19). (6) Select Port (Figure 20). (7) The default MQTT communication port is 1883; select TCP and enter 1883 under Specific local ports (Figure 21). (8) Select Allow the connection (Figure 22). (9) Select Domain, Private, and Public (Figure 23). (10) Enter a rule name and complete the outbound Port 1883 rule (Figure 24). (11) Confirm that the inbound rule is enabled normally (Figure 25). Configure IP and Router location: before starting an experiment, connect to Wi-Fi, select ST_route, and edit the network IP settings (Figure 26). Figure 26, Network IP Settings."@en ;
    sth:mqtt_communication_setting_method "Before using MQTT, add an inbound rule in Windows Defender Firewall to allow TCP Port 1883 communication so that the computer can enable the MQTT service."@en ;
    sth:mqtt_communication_setting_query sth:mqtt_communication_settings ;
    sth:mqtt_default_communication_port "1883" ;
    sth:mqtt_default_connection_port "1883" .

